---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro'

const repo = 'https://github.com/YuraTadevosyan/three-js-and-animations'
const index = 'https://yuratadevosyan.github.io/three-js-and-animations/'

const gazeTraits = [
  { title: 'Tracks', body: 'Pupils aim at the cursor itself, not at a bounding box, so the gaze stays honest at any angle.' },
  { title: 'Dilates', body: 'Pupils open in the dark and when you move fast, and contract in full daylight.' },
  { title: 'Blinks', body: 'On its own schedule, in clusters, and at anything you click.' },
  { title: 'Searches', body: 'Leave the window and the eyes saccade around looking for where you went.' },
  { title: 'Sags', body: 'Lids drop as the page gets drowsy, and close when it falls asleep.' },
  { title: 'Leans', body: 'Buttons within reach pull toward the cursor before you arrive at them.' },
]
---

<Base
  title="Living Website"
  description="A page that behaves like an organism — it breathes, watches your cursor, grows a garden between visits, changes weather, and follows a real day/night cycle."
>
  <living-sky></living-sky>
  <cursor-aura></cursor-aura>

  <a
    href="#breath"
    class="sr-only focus:not-sr-only focus:absolute focus:left-4 focus:top-4 focus:z-50 focus:rounded-full focus:bg-primary focus:px-4 focus:py-2 focus:text-primary-foreground"
  >
    Skip to the systems
  </a>

  <header class="mx-auto flex max-w-6xl items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 pt-7">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
      <pulse-badge></pulse-badge>
      <voice-toggle compact></voice-toggle>
    </div>
    <nav class="flex items-center gap-5 text-xs text-muted-foreground">
      <a class="hover:text-foreground" href={index}>All showcases</a>
      <a class="hover:text-foreground" href={repo} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Source</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <main class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6">
    <!-- ───────────────────────────── hero ───────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="flex min-h-[78svh] flex-col justify-center py-20">
      <p class="eyebrow">Astro · Lit · Motion · PixiJS</p>

      <living-title
        class="mt-5 block max-w-4xl text-[clamp(2.75rem,9vw,6.5rem)] font-semibold leading-[0.95] tracking-tight text-balance"
      >
        This website is alive.
      </living-title>

      <p class="mt-8 max-w-xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
        Not animated — alive. One heartbeat drives everything that moves here.
        The sky breathes, the interface follows your cursor, the garden further
        down keeps growing — and cross-breeding — while this tab is closed, the
        weather drifts on its own, and the colour of every element on screen is
        a function of the actual time where you are sitting.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4">
        <living-button eyes>
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#breath"><span>See what's alive</span></a>
        </living-button>
        <living-button>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#colophon"><span>How it's built</span></a>
        </living-button>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-14 max-w-lg text-sm text-muted-foreground/80">
        Nothing here is on a timeline. Leave it alone and it gets drowsy, then
        sleeps, then dreams. Switch tabs and it carries on in the favicon. Come
        back tomorrow and the plants will have grown without you. It has a
        voice too, but it stays quiet until you ask.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ──────────────────────────── 01 breath ───────────────────────── -->
    <section id="breath" data-reveal class="scroll-mt-20 border-t border-border/60 py-24">
      <div class="grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-12">
        <div class="lg:col-span-5">
          <p class="eyebrow">01 — Respiration</p>
          <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">It breathes</h2>
          <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
            <p>
              A single oscillator runs the whole page. The horizon in the sky
              shader lifts on the inhale, cards ease forward, buttons swell by
              half a percent, and the headline's letterforms soften — all from
              the same number.
            </p>
            <p>
              The curve is asymmetric on purpose. A real inhale takes about
              forty percent of the cycle and the exhale takes the rest, with a
              brief hold at the top. Make it a clean sine wave and it stops
              reading as breathing and starts reading as a pulsing div.
            </p>
            <p>
              The rate is not fixed either. Move around and it climbs toward
              sixteen breaths a minute; sit still and it settles to seven; let
              it fall asleep and it drops to five.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <organ-card class="organ p-6 sm:p-8 lg:col-span-7">
          <vitals-panel></vitals-panel>
        </organ-card>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ─────────────────────────── 02 attention ─────────────────────── -->
    <section id="attention" data-reveal class="scroll-mt-20 border-t border-border/60 py-24">
      <div class="max-w-2xl">
        <p class="eyebrow">02 — Attention</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">It watches you</h2>
        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
          <p>
            Cursor position is the page's only sense organ, so a lot is made of
            it. Every eye on this page shares one pointer stream and one set of
            springs; what varies is where each element sits, which is enough to
            make six faces look like six creatures rather than six copies.
          </p>
          <p>
            The page also measures how much you are moving, not just where.
            That figure drives the breath rate, the pupils, the strength of the
            glow around your cursor, and how long it takes to get drowsy.
          </p>
          <p>
            Stop entirely and it works through the stages: alert, awake, drowsy
            at twenty-two seconds, asleep at fifty-five. Then it starts to
            dream — a value that builds over about fifteen seconds of sleep and
            is handed to the sky shader, which slowly abandons the real palette
            for a cosine gradient that no sky has ever been. Move the cursor
            and it collapses almost instantly. It should never look like the
            page is still hallucinating once you are obviously back.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-10 rounded-[var(--radius)] border border-border/60 bg-card/30 py-14">
        <living-eyes size="150" label="A large pair of eyes following the cursor"></living-eyes>
        <living-eyes size="88"></living-eyes>
        <living-eyes size="56"></living-eyes>
      </div>

      <ul class="mt-10 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3" role="list">
        {
          gazeTraits.map((trait) => (
            <li data-reveal-item>
              <organ-card class="organ flex h-full items-start gap-4 p-5" tilt="6">
                <living-eyes size="46"></living-eyes>
                <div>
                  <h3 class="font-semibold tracking-tight">{trait.title}</h3>
                  <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-muted-foreground text-pretty">{trait.body}</p>
                </div>
              </organ-card>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4">
        <living-button eyes>
          <button class="btn btn-primary" type="button"><span>This one looks around</span></button>
        </living-button>
        <living-button eyes>
          <button class="btn btn-ghost" type="button"><span>So does this one</span></button>
        </living-button>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
          Both pull toward your cursor from about 170 pixels away.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ──────────────────────────── 03 growth ───────────────────────── -->
    <section id="growth" data-reveal class="scroll-mt-20 border-t border-border/60 py-24">
      <div class="grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-12">
        <div class="lg:col-span-5">
          <p class="eyebrow">03 — Growth &amp; heredity</p>
          <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
            It grows, and it breeds
          </h2>
          <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
            <p>
              Every plant carries a genome: sixteen heritable traits covering
              species, segment length, branching angle, curl, taper, leaf
              spacing, flower size, hue shift and vigour. The whole shape is
              grown from those numbers. A seed is still stored alongside, but
              all it does now is supply the jitter that stops a plant looking
              mechanically symmetrical — same genome, same seed, same plant.
            </p>
            <p>
              Growth is real time, not a loop. Plants photosynthesise faster in
              daylight, drink when it rains, wilt in drought, and slow down but
              never stop at night. Around fifteen minutes of good conditions
              takes a seedling to maturity. Then they flower, seed, and fade
              back into the soil, raising the fertility for whatever grows next.
            </p>
            <p>
              What happens at the flowering stage depends on who visits. Bees
              and butterflies work the bed by day, moths after dark; none of
              them fly in rain or a strong wind. They navigate to open flowers
              rather than drifting, and they prefer a plant they aren't already
              carrying pollen from — so pollen genuinely crosses the bed.
            </p>
            <p>
              A plant that has been pollinated produces a seed whose genome is
              a cross: each trait independently dominant from one parent,
              dominant from the other, or intermediate, then nudged. One left
              alone selfs instead, drifting slightly, so a garden nobody visits
              still changes over generations. Ferns sit the whole thing out —
              they have no flowers, so nothing comes to them.
            </p>
            <p>
              The calendar reaches the bed too, and as a cross-fade rather than
              a switch: four season weights that sum to one, so mid-October is
              mostly autumn with summer still bleeding through. Growth runs at
              about half speed in midwinter and a fifth above normal in spring.
              Canopies turn gold on a per-plant delay, so the change moves
              through the bed over weeks instead of happening at once, and the
              leaves that come off drift on the same wind the clouds are using.
              Snow lies on the soil while it falls and melts back into the
              moisture budget when the temperature climbs.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="lg:col-span-7">
          <garden-bed></garden-bed>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ───────────────────────────── 04 sky ─────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="sky" data-reveal class="scroll-mt-20 border-t border-border/60 py-24">
      <div class="grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-12">
        <div class="lg:col-span-5">
          <p class="eyebrow">04 — Weather &amp; sun</p>
          <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The sky moves on</h2>
          <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
            <p>
              Weather walks a Markov chain. Every ninety seconds to three and a
              half minutes the current system rolls for a successor, weighted so
              it can only move to something adjacent — clear never jumps
              straight to storm, it has to get there through fair, cloudy and
              rain. That constraint is the whole reason the sky feels continuous
              instead of shuffled.
            </p>
            <p>
              Today's real date picks the season, which re-weights the table:
              snow is common in winter and impossible in summer, storms peak in
              July, mist belongs to autumn. Aurora only rolls after dark.
            </p>
            <p>
              The sun runs on the standard sunrise equation against the real
              calendar day, so the night is genuinely long in December and short
              in June. Everything you see is a crossfade — no system ever cuts.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <organ-card class="organ p-6 sm:p-8 lg:col-span-7" tilt="3">
          <sky-controls></sky-controls>
          <div class="rule mt-7"></div>
          <div class="mt-6">
            <p class="eyebrow">Voice</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
              All of this can be heard as well as seen. Wind is filtered noise
              whose band and level track the real wind vector; rain is the same
              noise through a highpass, scaled by precipitation; thunder fires
              on the same event the lightning does, as a burst with a filter
              sweeping down over two and a half seconds. Crickets chirp at a
              rate taken from Dolbear's law, which really is a thermometer.
              Birds arrive within an hour of your actual sunrise. Underneath it
              all is a sub-bass swell on the breath.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-4">
              <voice-toggle></voice-toggle>
            </div>
          </div>
        </organ-card>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ──────────────────────────── 05 colour ───────────────────────── -->
    <section id="colour" data-reveal class="scroll-mt-20 border-t border-border/60 py-24">
      <div class="max-w-2xl">
        <p class="eyebrow">05 — Circadian colour</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">It keeps your hours</h2>
        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
          <p>
            There is no dark mode here, and no <code class="font-mono text-[0.85em] text-foreground">dark:</code>
            variant anywhere in the markup. Seven palettes are pinned around the
            clock — deep night, first light, sunrise, midday, golden hour, dusk
            — and the current time is interpolated between the two it falls
            between, hue by hue, on the short way round the wheel.
          </p>
          <p>
            The result is written straight onto <code class="font-mono text-[0.85em] text-foreground">:root</code>
            as the same HSL custom properties Tailwind compiles against. So
            <code class="font-mono text-[0.85em] text-foreground">bg-primary</code> is a different green at
            noon than it is at nine in the evening, and every component follows
            without knowing anything about it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Writing custom properties on the root element invalidates style for
            the entire document, so this runs at eight hertz rather than sixty,
            and skips any value that has not changed. The sun does not move
            fast enough for anyone to notice the difference.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10">
        <palette-strip></palette-strip>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
        Drag the sun slider in the previous section to watch these move.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ─────────────────────────── 06 colophon ──────────────────────── -->
    <section id="colophon" data-reveal class="scroll-mt-20 border-t border-border/60 py-24">
      <div class="max-w-2xl">
        <p class="eyebrow">06 — Anatomy</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">One heartbeat</h2>
        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
          <p>
            The rule that makes this hang together: there is exactly one
            <code class="font-mono text-[0.85em] text-foreground">requestAnimationFrame</code> loop on the
            page, and every system subscribes to it. CSS animations and
            per-component loops each run on their own timeline and drift apart
            within seconds — which is precisely why most animated pages read as
            a pile of unrelated widgets rather than one thing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Motion is used only for transitions that have a beginning and an
            end: scroll reveals, the notice that appears when you come back, the
            caption under the garden. Continuous motion belongs to the
            heartbeat. One writer per property, always.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
        <organ-card class="organ p-5" tilt="5" data-reveal-item>
          <h3 class="font-semibold tracking-tight">Three substrates</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
            A WebGL sky in PixiJS, a garden in SVG, and the interface in plain
            DOM — all reading the same state object on the same frame.
          </p>
        </organ-card>

        <organ-card class="organ p-5" tilt="5" data-reveal-item>
          <h3 class="font-semibold tracking-tight">Light-DOM web components</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
            Lit elements render into themselves rather than a shadow root, so
            one Tailwind sheet and one set of CSS variables reach every organ.
          </p>
        </organ-card>

        <organ-card class="organ p-5" tilt="5" data-reveal-item>
          <h3 class="font-semibold tracking-tight">Zero binary assets</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
            Clouds, rain, snow and sun flares are drawn into offscreen canvases
            at boot, and every sound is synthesized from noise and oscillators.
            Nothing is fetched and nothing pops in.
          </p>
        </organ-card>

        <organ-card class="organ p-5" tilt="5" data-reveal-item>
          <h3 class="font-semibold tracking-tight">Alive in the tab</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
            The favicon is a canvas redrawn every two seconds with the live sky
            and the sun at its real position. It runs on an interval rather
            than the heartbeat, because the heartbeat correctly stops when the
            document is hidden — which is exactly when this matters.
          </p>
        </organ-card>

        <organ-card class="organ p-5" tilt="5" data-reveal-item>
          <h3 class="font-semibold tracking-tight">Organs sleep off-screen</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
            Each element only ticks while it intersects the viewport, so an
            idle tab costs one shader draw instead of nine live systems.
          </p>
        </organ-card>

        <organ-card class="organ p-5" tilt="5" data-reveal-item>
          <h3 class="font-semibold tracking-tight">It degrades quietly</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
            No WebGL leaves a correct CSS gradient. No local storage starts a
            fresh garden. No JavaScript at all still leaves a readable page.
          </p>
        </organ-card>

        <organ-card class="organ p-5" tilt="5" data-reveal-item>
          <h3 class="font-semibold tracking-tight">It respects the setting</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
            Under <code class="font-mono text-[0.85em] text-foreground">prefers-reduced-motion</code> the
            rhythm keeps running so timings stay correct, but the amplitude
            collapses to nothing.
          </p>
        </organ-card>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pb-16 pt-8">
    <div class="rule"></div>
    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-5 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      <p>
        Open the console and poke at <code class="font-mono text-foreground">window.organism</code>.
        Everything on this page is one readable state object.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-5">
        <a class="hover:text-foreground" href={index}>Showcase index</a>
        <a class="hover:text-foreground" href={repo} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </footer>
</Base>
